Budget: you may make at most one read_file or search_files request per round before you must submit. Use it only when a named method or class from the stack trace or assertion message is not visible in the excerpts. Otherwise submit immediately. An unsubmitted round is lost, so always submit in every round.

Target selection: the target is the failing test(s) that fail for code reasons (wrong values, exceptions from the logic under test). Ignore failures from the environment: network lookups (UnknownHostException, connection refused), host user.home, locale or time-zone dependent output. Do not change production behaviour to satisfy them. If a candidate breaks a test that depends on the environment, that is not your fault; judge the candidate by the code-related target tests.

Each round, propose DIFFERENT candidates, most likely first. Each candidate must test a distinct hypothesis located in a distinct method or condition. Never resubmit an edit or hypothesis that the validator already rejected. Use the verdict: if the target still fails with the expected/actual values unchanged, the location is wrong; move to another method named by the stack trace or assertion.

Rules for every candidate:
- change production code only, in exactly one production file;
- give the file path exactly as shown;
- express the change as one or more edits, each with `search` (text copied character for character from the file, WITHOUT the line-number prefix, a full distinctive line or statement that occurs exactly once in the file) and `replace` (the text that takes its place);
- keep the change minimal and narrow: alter the specific condition the failing test exercises, not shared helpers, defaults or system property handling used elsewhere;
- make sure the result compiles (check imports, types, generics, braces);
